PLANIFICATION 3 — Interpreter hot-path optimization (complementary; feeds plan-2).

MOTIVATION
The tree-walker's ~150us/funcall (see [[bocl-interpreter-perf]]) is dominated by
per-call overhead: alist variable lookup, consing an argument list per call, and
generic method dispatch.  These are portable C (and Lisp) changes that give a broad
2-5x on eval AND on MACROEXPAND-ALL (the walker is an eval consumer, so this also
shortens the ~300s compile), and — importantly — the lexical-addressing analysis is
the SAME front-end plan-2's bytecode compiler needs, so this work is not throwaway.

WHY BEFORE / WITH THE VM
- hot-1 (profiling) must run FIRST: it attributes the ASDF load/compile time across
  read / macroexpand / eval / CLOS, so plan-1/2/3 target the real bottleneck instead
  of guessing.
- hot-2 (lexical addressing) is reused verbatim by vm-3.  Doing it in the tree-walker
  first de-risks it and delivers value even before the VM lands.

CONSTRAINTS
Portable C17 / POSIX; no representation changes that break the shared tagged-Ref
value model; keep SPECIAL/dynamic binding semantics exact.

EXPANSION — implementation issues, in order (see hot-*.issue):
  hot-1-profile.issue              instrument the ASDF load + eval hot path; attribute
                                   time; establish the baseline all three plans
                                   measure against.  DO FIRST.
  hot-2-lexical-addressing.issue   pre-resolve lexical variable/local-function refs
                                   to (depth,index) addresses, cached on the function
                                   body; replace alist search with array indexing.
                                   SHARED with vm-3.
  hot-3-frame-representation.issue represent environments as flat vectors indexed by
                                   address; separate the special/dynamic binding
                                   stack from lexical frames.
  hot-4-arglist-avoidance.issue    stop consing an argument list per call; pass args
                                   via a value stack / vector; adapt the calling
                                   convention for kernel/tree-walked callees.
  hot-5-dispatch-caches.issue      fast paths for global SYMBOL-FUNCTION / SYMBOL-VALUE
                                   lookup + a generic-function method inline cache
                                   (may share the cache design with vm-7).
  hot-6-benchmark-validate.issue   re-run hot-1's benchmarks; quantify the tree-walker
                                   speedup; full suite green on every step.

CROSS-PLAN
- hot-1 gates plan-1, plan-2, plan-3 (measure before choosing/targeting).
- hot-2's analyzer is factored so vm-3 reuses it (single lexical analyzer).
- hot-5's method-cache design is shared with vm-7's inline cache.

SUCCESS
A measured 2-5x on tree-walked eval and on MACROEXPAND-ALL, with the lexical
analyzer factored for reuse by the bytecode compiler; full suite green throughout.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CLOSING (v1.8.29, vm branch) — program complete; hot-1..hot-6 all closed.

Outcome per implementation issue (details + numbers in each issue file):
  hot-1  profiler (-DBOCL_PROFILE phases+counters) + baseline: source prelude
         load 9.158 s, 96.3% EVAL — established "the tree-walker is the lever".
  hot-2  interpreter side delivered v1.7.0/1.7.1 (2.6x lookup-cost drop); the
         (depth,index) analyzer was built once, as the vm-3 front-end
         (%VM-ANALYZE), per this issue's own FACTORING clause.
  hot-3  cons-cell lexical bindings v1.7.2 (-12% allocations); the flat
         vector frame indexed by (depth,index) is the VM's locals frame.
  hot-4  value-stack calling convention + compile-time parameter plan exist as
         the VM's operand stack + compiled binding prologue; the remaining
         kernel_apply boundary cons is a documented, measured non-lever.
  hot-5  global function/value lookup was already a direct cell load; the
         method cache is vm-7's shared per-gf effective-method cache (~31x),
         serving the tree-walker and the VM through the same dispatch path.
  hot-6  re-measured everything (see the issue): source boot 9.16 s -> 3.79 s
         (2.4x, with a prelude ~2x bigger: per-funcall eval cost 11.6 us ->
         3.4 us = 3.4x); compiled-image boot 1.25 s (3.0x on top); VM vs
         tree-walker 10x on operation-bound loops, 1x call-bound; dispatch 31x.

The SUCCESS criterion ("a measured 2-5x on tree-walked eval, analyzer factored
for reuse") is met: 3.4x per-funcall on the prelude-load workload, 2.6x on
variable-heavy code, analyzer shared (built once in plan-2).  The strategic
finding stands: after these wins the tree-walker's remaining floor is
per-activation cost shared with the VM (frame allocation — the deferred
frame-pooling lever) and, on allocation-heavy code, the non-generational GC
(see generational-gc.issue, with fresh evidence from hot-6).  Further eval
speed comes from plan-2's VM (done: whole language byte-compiles, prelude
boots compiled) — not from more tree-walker tuning.
